  [1mApproaches[0m

  All three assume Sentry via CDN, errors + masked breadcrumbs, browser only. They differ in [1mhow app code reaches the [0m
  [1mlogger[0m, which determines vendor lock-in and testability.

  [1mA. Logger façade module (recommended).[0m A new [38;5;153mlogger.js[39m loads before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m and exposes a tiny surface — [38;5;153mlog.event()[39m,
  [38;5;153mlog.warn()[39m, [38;5;153mlog.error()[39m. It owns Sentry init, the masking config, environment detection, and a no-op fallback when the
  CDN is blocked. [38;5;153mapp.js[39m never references [38;5;153mSentry[39m.
  [3mBuys:[0m one place to audit for redaction — the thing most likely to leak a password; swapping vendors touches one file;
  call sites are readable. [3mCosts:[0m one more file and one more indirection. [3mRules out:[0m nothing.

  [1mB. Direct SDK at call sites.[0m [38;5;153mSentry.init()[39m inline in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m, then [38;5;153mSentry.captureException[39m / [38;5;153mSentry.addBreadcrumb[39m
  sprinkled through [38;5;153mapp.js[39m.
  [3mBuys:[0m least code, full SDK surface always available, nothing to maintain. [3mCosts:[0m redaction discipline lives at every
  call site rather than one; [38;5;153mapp.js[39m becomes untestable without the real SDK loaded; changing vendors is a rewrite. [3mWins
[0m  [3mwhen:[0m you're certain this is throwaway instrumentation.

  [1mC. Boundary instrumentation.[0m Don't touch [38;5;153mlogin()[39m or [38;5;153mvalidateForm()[39m at all. Wrap the submit handler and install global
  [38;5;153merror[39m / [38;5;153munhandledrejection[39m handlers in one place; everything else is inferred from breadcrumbs.
  [3mBuys:[0m near-zero edits to business logic, no logging noise in the functions. [3mCosts:[0m you lose the domain events — a
  validation failure and a login rejection look the same from outside the boundary, and those are the two failures you'd
  actually be chasing.

  I recommend [1mA[0m, folding in C's global handlers (they belong in the façade's init, not scattered). B's simplicity isn't
  worth giving up a single auditable redaction point in a file that handles passwords.

